## 🧪 Testing & Debugging

### Test Files

The project includes several test scripts:

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| [test_agent.py](file:///C:/Users/lenovo/Downloads/MIRA-Deliverables/MIRA-Windows/test_agent.py) | Test agent without UI |
| [test_context.py](file:///C:/Users/lenovo/Downloads/MIRA-Deliverables/MIRA-Windows/test_context.py) | Test conversation memory |
| [test_smtp.py](file:///C:/Users/lenovo/Downloads/MIRA-Deliverables/MIRA-Windows/test_smtp.py) | Test email sending |
| [test_tool_memory.py](file:///C:/Users/lenovo/Downloads/MIRA-Deliverables/MIRA-Windows/test_tool_memory.py) | Test tool execution memory |

### Running Tests

```bash
# Test agent directly
python test_agent.py

# Test email functionality
python test_smtp.py
```

### Debugging Tips

**1. Check MCP Server Logs**

The MCP server prints detailed logs:
```
[MCP] Received agent_invoke with model=mistral:7b, input=Add task...
[MCP] Clearing AGENT history (had 12 messages)
```

**2. Check Tool Execution**

Add debug prints in connectors:
```python
@register_tool(name="todo.add", ...)
def add_task(task: str):
    print(f"[TODO] Adding task: {task}")  # Debug
    # ... rest of code
```

**3. Test Tools Directly**

```python
# In Python REPL
from mcp_server.tools import execute_tool

result = execute_tool("todo.add", {"task": "Test", "priority": "low"})
print(result)
```

**4. Monitor WebSocket Communication**

Use browser DevTools → Network → WS to see messages between UI and MCP server.

